<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>TTextHighlighter</h1>
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TTextHighlighter" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TTextHighlighter</tt> highlights its body content, which covers static text and the
rendering results of its child controls. The control renders a <tt>&lt;pre&gt;</tt>
element holding a <tt>&lt;code&gt;</tt> element, and the
<a href="https://highlightjs.org/">highlight.js</a> library colors the block in the
browser.
</p>

<h2>Choosing a language</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>Language</tt> names the syntax. It defaults to <tt>php</tt>. The control lowercases
the value and writes it as the class of the rendered <tt>&lt;code&gt;</tt> element. The
library ships 185 language definitions, and highlights a block whose class names one of
them.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
A value the library does not recognize leaves it to detect the language from the content.
That is what happens to <tt>prado</tt>, which the quickstart uses throughout for control
templates. The library has no PRADO definition, detects the content as XML, and colors it
as markup.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TTextHighlighter Language="sql"&gt;
  SELECT name FROM users WHERE id = 1
&lt;/com:TTextHighlighter&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Presentation</h2>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Default</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>ShowLineNumbers</tt></td>
	<td>false</td>
	<td>Puts a line number in front of each line. The <tt>hljs-line-numbers</tt> CSS class styles them.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>EnableCopyCode</tt></td>
	<td>false</td>
	<td>Adds a <tt>Copy code</tt> button in the corner of the block that copies the text to the clipboard.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TabSize</tt></td>
	<td>4</td>
	<td>The number of spaces a tab character becomes.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>SyntaxStyle</tt></td>
	<td><tt>default</tt></td>
	<td>Names one of the highlight.js color themes. The control publishes the matching stylesheet.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
The control registers a stylesheet during pre-render, so the page template needs a
<a href="?page=Controls.Head">THead</a> control for the highlighting to appear.
</p>

<h2>Encoding the body content</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>EncodeHtml</tt> defaults to true. The control HTML-encodes the body content before
rendering it, and strips leading whitespace so the block does not open with a blank
line. Setting it to false renders the body content untouched, which a page needs
only when the content is already encoded.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
To use <tt>TTextHighlighter</tt>, simply enclose the contents to be syntax highlighted within the body of a <tt>TTextHighlighter</tt> control. The following example highlights a piece of PHP code,
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TTextHighlighter ShowLineNumbers="true"&gt;
<?php
$str = 'one|two|three|four';
print_r(explode('|', $str, 2)); // will output an array
&lt;/com:TTextHighlighter&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

